&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


ไอเดียที่ทำให้มันต่าง
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ตารางบน README อธิบายสิ่งที่โปรเจกต์นี้ให้ใน 6 ข้อ [1] และข้อที่ผมคิดว่าสำคัญที่สุดคือ &lt;strong&gt;Reproducible experiments&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;README เขียนว่า &lt;em&gt;"ติดตาม variant ต่าง ๆ ในต้นไม้การทดลองที่เป็น git-native ทุกรอบการรันจะได้ archive ที่แก้ไขไม่ได้ของ commit ที่บันทึกไว้"&lt;/em&gt; [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;คำว่า &lt;strong&gt;immutable archive&lt;/strong&gt; กับ &lt;strong&gt;git-native&lt;/strong&gt; ในประโยคเดียวกันคือสิ่งที่ทำให้ผมสนใจ&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;เพราะปัญหาที่ทีมวิจัยด้วย AI มักเจอไม่ใช่การหาคำตอบ แต่คือการตอบคำถามว่า &lt;strong&gt;ผลลัพธ์ที่ได้เมื่อวาน มาจากโค้ดเวอร์ชันไหน&lt;/strong&gt; เมื่อ agent แก้โค้ดเองหลายรอบในวันเดียว&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;และข้อที่เกี่ยวข้องกันคือ &lt;strong&gt;Evidence in context&lt;/strong&gt; ซึ่งเก็บ log, diff, ไฟล์, ผลลัพธ์ และ artifact ไว้ติดกับงานที่สร้างมัน [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


แยก agent ออกจากกันจริง
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ข้อแรกในตารางคือ &lt;strong&gt;Parallel exploration&lt;/strong&gt; ซึ่ง README อธิบายว่า &lt;em&gt;"ให้แต่ละทิศทางการวิจัยมี session ของ agent แยกอิสระ และ git worktree ที่แยกจากกัน"&lt;/em&gt; [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;การเลือกใช้ &lt;strong&gt;worktree แยกกัน&lt;/strong&gt; เป็นรายละเอียดทางเทคนิคที่ผมคิดว่าคนทำงานกับ agent ควรสังเกต&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;เพราะวิธีที่คนส่วนใหญ่ทำตอนนี้คือให้ agent หลายตัวแก้ไฟล์ชุดเดียวกัน ซึ่งทำให้เกิดความขัดแย้งที่ต้องมานั่งแก้ทีหลัง การแยก worktree ตัดปัญหานั้นตั้งแต่ต้น&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


ทำงานแบบอัตโนมัติเต็มวง
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;README ระบุว่ามีโหมดที่เรียกว่า &lt;strong&gt;Autoresearch&lt;/strong&gt; ซึ่งรันวงจรเต็มได้เอง คือเสนอไอเดีย แก้โค้ด สั่งรันการทดลอง ตรวจหลักฐาน และตัดสินใจว่าจะลองอะไรต่อ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;และรองรับ agent หลายตัวสำรวจคนละทิศทางพร้อมกัน โดยต้นไม้การทดลองยังเก็บสายสัมพันธ์ของแต่ละเส้นทางไว้ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;จุดนี้เป็นทั้งข้อดีและสิ่งที่ต้องระวัง เพราะการให้ agent ตัดสินใจเองทั้งวงจรหมายความว่าคุณต้องไว้ใจกลไกการเก็บหลักฐานมากกว่าจะไว้ใจผลลัพธ์&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


เรื่องที่ผมให้คะแนนมากที่สุด
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


ขอบเขตข้อมูลที่ระบุชัด
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ส่วนที่ผมคิดว่าทีมนี้ทำได้ดีและควรเป็นตัวอย่างให้โปรเจกต์อื่น คือความชัดเจนเรื่องข้อมูลออกนอกเครื่อง [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;README เขียนว่า OpenResearch รันบน &lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1&lt;/code&gt; ด้วยฐานข้อมูล SQLite ในเครื่อง และ &lt;strong&gt;การสร้างโปรเจกต์หรือเริ่มการรันไม่เผยแพร่โค้ดของคุณ&lt;/strong&gt; [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;บิลด์ที่เป็นทางการยังระบุตรง ๆ ว่าส่ง &lt;strong&gt;เหตุการณ์การใช้งานแบบ opt-out&lt;/strong&gt; ที่ผูกกับรหัสการติดตั้งแบบสุ่ม&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;และระบุว่าเหตุการณ์เหล่านั้น &lt;strong&gt;ไม่รวมโค้ด, prompt, เนื้อหาไฟล์, path, ชื่อ repository, token, อีเมล หรือรหัสโปรเจกต์และการทดลอง&lt;/strong&gt; [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;คำสั่ง &lt;code&gt;orx telemetry off&lt;/code&gt; ปิดการส่งได้เองถ้าไม่ต้องการ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ผมมองว่าการระบุขอบเขตของสิ่งที่เก็บแบบนี้ โปรเจกต์จำนวนมากยังทำไม่ครบ และมันช่วยให้คนตัดสินใจได้ว่าควรใช้หรือไม่&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


สิ่งที่ต้องรู้ก่อนใช้
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


สองคำเตือนที่ควรอ่าน
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;มีสองจุดที่ README เขียนไว้และควรอ่านให้ครบ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;หนึ่ง&lt;/strong&gt; การใช้โหมด &lt;code&gt;--remote-host&lt;/code&gt; เพื่อรันบนเครื่องที่มี GPU แล้วเปิด dashboard จากแล็ปท็อป README ระบุว่า บริการระยะไกลผูกกับ loopback และ &lt;strong&gt;ไม่มีการยืนยันตัวตนระดับแอปพลิเคชัน&lt;/strong&gt; ดังนั้นผู้ใช้คนอื่นบนโฮสต์นั้นเข้าถึงได้ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;นี่คือคำเตือนที่ต้องอ่าน เพราะมันหมายความว่าบริการนี้ไม่มีกลไกยืนยันตัวตนในตัว คุณจึงต้องดูแลการควบคุมการเข้าถึงเอง — อย่าเปิดบนโฮสต์ที่ใช้ร่วมกับคนอื่นโดยไม่ระวัง&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;สอง&lt;/strong&gt; บน Windows ยังเป็น beta และต้องมี Git for Windows ติดตั้งอยู่ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


ข้อควรระวัง
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;หนึ่ง&lt;/strong&gt; ข้อมูลทั้งหมดในบทความนี้มาจากหน้า GitHub และ README ของโปรเจกต์ ณ วันที่ 13 กันยายน 2026 [1] ผมไม่ได้ติดตั้งหรือทดลองใช้ OpenResearch จึงยืนยันไม่ได้ว่าทำงานได้ตามที่อธิบาย&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;สอง&lt;/strong&gt; ตัวเลข 1,863 ดาว และ 135 ฟอร์กเปลี่ยนได้ทุกวัน โปรดตรวจสอบล่าสุดก่อนนำไปอ้างอิง [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;สาม&lt;/strong&gt; ผมยังไม่ได้ทดสอบโหมด Autoresearch ที่ให้ agent ทำงานอัตโนมัติทั้งวงจร จึงไม่ยืนยันว่าการเก็บหลักฐานทำงานได้จริงในทุกกรณี&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;สี่&lt;/strong&gt; การเลือก agent รองรับ Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode และ Cursor ในระดับหนึ่ง แต่ผมไม่มีข้อมูลว่าความสามารถต่างกันแค่ไหนเมื่อใช้ agent คนละตัว [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ห้า&lt;/strong&gt; ผมไม่มีส่วนเกี่ยวข้องกับทีม alphaXiv และไม่เคยใช้บริการ openresearch.sh ซึ่งโปรเจกต์นี้มีโหมดที่ใช้ compute แบบ managed อยู่ด้วย การประเมินของผมจึงมาจากเอกสารบน GitHub เท่านั้น [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


สรุป
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenResearch น่าสนใจเพราะมันแก้ปัญหาที่คนมักมองข้าม คือ &lt;strong&gt;ความสามารถในการย้อนกลับไปดูว่าผลลัพธ์มาจากอะไร&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;การแยก worktree ต่อ agent และการเก็บ archive ที่แก้ไขไม่ได้ของทุกการรัน เป็นวิธีที่ตรงไปตรงมา และเป็นวิธีที่ทีมที่ทำงานกับ agent หลายตัวน่าจะเจ็บปวดกับปัญหานี้มาแล้ว&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ส่วนเรื่องความเป็นส่วนตัวที่ README เขียนไว้ชัดเจน ผมคิดว่าควรยกเป็นตัวอย่างให้โปรเจกต์อื่นทำตาม&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;คำถามที่ผมคิดว่าควรถามคือ งานที่คุณให้ agent ทำอยู่ตอนนี้ ถ้าอีกสามวันมีคนถามว่า "ผลนี้มาจากโค้ดเวอร์ชันไหน" คุณตอบได้ไหม&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ถ้าตอบไม่ได้ นั่นคือช่องที่เครื่องมือแบบนี้เข้ามาเติม และถ้าอยากลองด้วยตัวเอง เอกสารบน GitHub มีคำสั่งติดตั้งและคู่มือเริ่มต้นให้อ่านครบ [1]&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;หมายเหตุ:&lt;/strong&gt; บทความนี้เป็นชิ้นที่ 4 จาก 6 ชิ้นในชุดเดียวกัน ต่อจากเรื่อง Farcaster, Doop, Opik, GA4 Dashboards และ routing โมเดลสำหรับงานการตลาด&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


แหล่งอ้างอิง
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;[1] alphaXiv, "OpenResearch — Run parallel research agents with any model", GitHub (เข้าถึง 13 ก.ย. 2026), &lt;a href="https://github.com/alphaXiv/OpenResearch" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/alphaXiv/OpenResearch&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;European Union crypto regulation changed fundamentally with MiCA — the &lt;em&gt;Markets in Crypto-Assets&lt;/em&gt; Regulation. But the raw data behind it is deceptively messy. AssayDesk is a small, focused tool that turns five ESMA-published CSV registers into something you can actually reason about — without inventing facts along the way. Here's how it works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where the data comes from
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ESMA — the European Securities and Markets Authority — publishes five MiCA registers as CSV files:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Crypto-asset service providers (CASPs)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;E-money token (EMT) issuers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Asset-referenced token (ART) issuers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Crypto-asset white papers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Entities flagged as non-compliant by national competent authorities&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AssayDesk fetches those files on a &lt;strong&gt;daily schedule&lt;/strong&gt;. ESMA itself republishes roughly weekly, so most days the file is byte-identical and nothing changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every fetched file is archived with its SHA-256 hash &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; parsing. That means any figure on the site can be traced back to the exact bytes ESMA actually served — you're not taking anyone's word for it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What "as published" actually means
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The register tables store ESMA's values &lt;strong&gt;verbatim&lt;/strong&gt; — including truncated names, malformed dates, duplicated URLs, and blank fields. Nothing is silently repaired on the way in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a deliberate design choice: &lt;em&gt;if a value looks wrong here, it looks wrong in the source — and that is information in itself.&lt;/em&gt; A messy official register is itself a finding about how the data is being managed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One consequence worth knowing: ESMA publishes &lt;strong&gt;one CASP row per authorised service&lt;/strong&gt;, not per firm. So a single company appears once for each service it may provide. The sidebar counts entities; the register page shows the service rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What curation changes (and what it never does)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deterministic rules produce a &lt;strong&gt;display-ready value next to the raw one&lt;/strong&gt; — a repaired link, a normalised date, a trimmed name. The rules are versioned, and each correction records which rule produced it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The original value is always kept and always shown alongside. &lt;strong&gt;A correction is an annotation, never a replacement.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A weekly model review may add observations about suspicious records. It never writes a value — and any model output is labelled &lt;strong&gt;unverified until a human confirms it&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the change history can and cannot assert
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The change history records what AssayDesk's sync observed &lt;em&gt;between two published files&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a record appeared&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a field changed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a withdrawal date was published&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a record vanished&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are &lt;strong&gt;observations about the file — not statements about the firm&lt;/strong&gt;. The distinction matters:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A record disappearing from a CSV is &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; evidence that an authorisation was withdrawn. ESMA states withdrawals as a date column; disappearances are usually publication artefacts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Absence from the register is &lt;strong&gt;not by itself&lt;/strong&gt; proof that an entity is unauthorised — you should check the national register of the relevant member state.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why this matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most crypto-regulation dashboards quietly "fix" bad data and never tell you. AssayDesk does the opposite: it keeps the raw bytes, shows you exactly what it changed and why, and only annotates — never silently rewrites.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the real product: a trustworthy lens on an official but messy dataset, where provenance and honesty about uncertainty are the headline features, not an afterthought.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Start from the &lt;a href="https://assaydesk.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;overview&lt;/a&gt;, or read the &lt;a href="https://assaydesk.app/data-quality" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;data-quality report&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As our series continues, Deep-Dive Backend Systems Roadmap. I created the simplest weather API backend, calling the other API to get weather data and saving it in Redis for faster retrieval and to reduce API calls to the other service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I didn't think this design would fall apart so quickly. It took just a few requests for everything to go wrong. I knew this problem existed, but I never imagined it would show up this early. I will explain the issue in detail below in this blog post.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdd1m6x1i96xfyp9so94h.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdd1m6x1i96xfyp9so94h.png" alt="Weather api system design" width="483" height="380"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Basic setup for this:
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create a basic api which will act as a dummy weather api and send back JSON. So that we don't hit the rate limit of the actual weather api for this testing purpose.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handleWeatherJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 1. Read the JSON file&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"data.json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;WriteHeader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;StatusOK&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 2. Send file data in the response&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;io&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Copy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;HandleFunc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/api/weather"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handleWeatherJSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ListenAndServe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;":3000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;panic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now let's start building the actual system that serves users. For this example, I'm using a simple Weather API, but you can replace it with any third-party service, like an AI API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea is simple: whenever we call the third-party API, store its response in the cache. That way, if the user asks for the same data again or simply reloads the page, we can return the cached response instead of making another API call.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;callTheAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"city"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 1. Check Cache&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cachedBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rdb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Bytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Unmarshal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cachedBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewEncoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"Cache HIT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 2. Call Upstream API&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://localhost:3000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewDecoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Decode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 3. Save to Cache&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;dataBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Marshal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rdb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Minute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 4. Send Response&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewEncoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"Fetched from API"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 1. Setup Redis&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rdb&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;redis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;redis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Options&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Addr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="s"&gt;"localhost:6379"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Password&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 2. Handle API &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;HandleFunc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/weather"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;callTheAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ListenAndServe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;":8080"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;panic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For large-scale load testing, I wrote test cases in K6 to simulate 500 concurrent requests hitting this API at the same time.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;k6/http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;k6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;k6/metrics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Custom metrics to track cache hits and misses directly in the CLI summary&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheHits&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;cache_hits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheMisses&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;cache_misses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;options&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;scenarios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Scenario 1: Prime the cache with a single request at t = 0s&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;primer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;executor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;per-vu-iterations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;vus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;iterations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;startTime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;0s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;exec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;primeCache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Scenario 2: Stampede blast starting right at the expiration window&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// If TTL is 10s, start at 10.5s or 11s to guarantee the key has expired&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;stampede&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;executor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;shared-iterations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;vus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;               &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 500 concurrent goroutines/users&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;iterations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Total requests to blast&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;maxDuration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;10s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Window to complete them&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;startTime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;10.5s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Wait for the 10s TTL to cleanly expire&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;exec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;blastEndpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TARGET_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;http://localhost:8080/weather?city=nagpur&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Function for Scenario 1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;primeCache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;TARGET_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;primer status is 200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`[PRIMER] Cache primed at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Function for Scenario 2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;blastEndpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;TARGET_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Validate response status&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;check&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;status is 200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Track cache status from response headers&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Assumes your Go handler sends: w.Header().Set("X-Cache", "HIT" | "MISS")&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheHeader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;X-Cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheHeader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;HIT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheHits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheHeader&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;MISS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheMisses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And when I run this, I run into these issues. Most of the requests got missed by the cache.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Symptoms &amp;amp; Results:cache_hits.....................: 287 

cache_misses...................: 213
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And because of these 213 cache misses, all 213 requests ended up calling the external API. That created a huge number of unnecessary external requests and increased the API response time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why did this happen?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because all 500 requests arrived almost at the same time. There might be just a few milliseconds of difference, but the first 213 requests checked the cache, got &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;, and immediately called the external API. Each one fetched the exact same data and tried to store it in Redis. Meanwhile, the remaining 287 requests hit the cache after the data was already there and got the response directly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;BUT, BUT, BUT...&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just think about it for a second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;213 API requests hitting another external API at the exact same time. You'll hit the rate limit in the blink of an eye on a real Weather API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Or forget the Weather API. Imagine 213 identical database queries running at the same time. A simple database will slow down immediately, and a smaller one might even start struggling under that load.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the worst part? All 213 requests are doing the exact same work just to fetch the exact same data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is what you call, bro, the&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;THUNDERING HERD problem&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We have talked about this in more detail in another &lt;a href="https://dev.to/aniruddhagawali/surviving-the-storm-understanding-the-thundering-herd-problem-5e67"&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt;. You can check it out; right now will be talking about how to resolve it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the best solutions for this is Single Flight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Out of all 500 requests, only one request goes to the external API. The other 499 requests just wait on the server for that first request to finish.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once that request gets the response, every waiting request uses the same data instead of calling the external API again. We also store that response in the cache, so any future request can get it directly from Redis without hitting the external API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just write a simple single-flight program which will hold the requests until the data comes in, or use any single-flight library.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;callTheAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"city"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 1. Check Cache&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cachedBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rdb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Bytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Unmarshal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cachedBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewEncoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"Cache HIT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 2. Use SingleFlight to prevent concurrent duplicate API calls&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sfGroup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Do&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;interface&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;innerData&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// Call Upstream API&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://github.com"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewDecoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Decode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;innerData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// Save to Cache&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;dataBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Marshal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;innerData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rdb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Minute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;innerData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;// 3. Send Response&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NewEncoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"Fetched from API / SingleFlight"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ONLY FOR GO USERS&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One important thing: if you're saving data to Redis inside a Go goroutine, use a different background context, not the request context. The request context gets cancelled as soon as the request ends, so the Redis write can fail. A background context avoids that problem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Whenever you're caching data in Redis, always think about the Thundering Herd problem. Use Single Flight so only one request fetches the data, while the rest wait and reuse the same response instead of hitting the external API again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your MCP tools started failing a few weeks ago and nothing in your own code changed, you're not imagining it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The official Python &lt;code&gt;mcp&lt;/code&gt; package shipped 2.0.0 on 2026-07-28. It's a real major version, with public APIs removed and renamed. Latest on PyPI right now is 2.2.0, and a plain &lt;code&gt;pip install mcp&lt;/code&gt; resolves to 2.x. Any library that depends on &lt;code&gt;mcp&lt;/code&gt; without an upper bound quietly floated forward and broke at install time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I spent a while reproducing this in throwaway venvs with 1.29.1 and 2.2.0 side by side. Here's what actually changed, what the errors look like, and how to get moving again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1st, one name collision to get out of the way
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two SDKs share the name. Python &lt;code&gt;mcp&lt;/code&gt; is the one on PyPI. The Rust one is &lt;code&gt;rmcp&lt;/code&gt; on crates.io, which is what Goose and some other Rust agents use, and it's on 3.x now. If you're debugging a Rust agent, the Python 2.x story is not your story. I nearly chased the wrong version myself...... glad I checked the crate name first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1st break: streamablehttp_client is gone
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In 1.x:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mcp.client.streamable_http&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;streamablehttp_client&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;streamablehttp_client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sse_read_timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;get_session_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="bp"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In 2.x that import raises ImportError. The replacement is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mcp.client.streamable_http&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;streamable_http_client&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;streamable_http_client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="bp"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two changes there. The name has underscores now, and the context manager yields a 2-tuple. The 3rd element, &lt;code&gt;get_session_id&lt;/code&gt;, is gone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That 2nd part is the one that hurts, because it doesn't fail at import. The wrapper imports fine, then blows up at runtime, and you get the error a lot of people are probably googling right now:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;ValueError: not enough values to unpack (expected 3, got 2)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I reproduced that against a live streamable-HTTP server on 2.2.0. In the installed package it's &lt;code&gt;mcp/client/streamable_http.py&lt;/code&gt;, around line 753: &lt;code&gt;yield read_stream, write_stream&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2nd break: the transport kwargs are gone
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;StreamableHTTPTransport.__init__&lt;/code&gt; in 2.x is just &lt;code&gt;(self, url)&lt;/code&gt;. The &lt;code&gt;timeout&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sse_read_timeout&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;headers&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;auth&lt;/code&gt; keywords aren't accepted there anymore; set them on the httpx2 &lt;code&gt;AsyncClient&lt;/code&gt; instead. Pass them and you get &lt;code&gt;TypeError: unexpected keyword argument 'timeout'&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Honesty note in the other direction: &lt;code&gt;sse_client&lt;/code&gt; still takes &lt;code&gt;timeout&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;sse_read_timeout&lt;/code&gt;. So the "all the transport kwargs got dropped" version of this story is wrong. It's the streamable-HTTP path that changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3rd break: field names went snake_case
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the quiet one. The Pydantic protocol models renamed their attributes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;tool.inputSchema&lt;/code&gt; goes to &lt;code&gt;tool.input_schema&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;result.isError&lt;/code&gt; goes to &lt;code&gt;result.is_error&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;structuredContent&lt;/code&gt; goes to &lt;code&gt;structured_content&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;nextCursor&lt;/code&gt; goes to &lt;code&gt;next_cursor&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The JSON on the wire is still camelCase (the aliases keep that), so servers and clients still agree on the protocol. It's your Python that breaks, with an AttributeError. Bonus trap: &lt;code&gt;model_dump()&lt;/code&gt; without &lt;code&gt;by_alias=True&lt;/code&gt; now hands you snake_case dicts without complaint, which can poison anything downstream that expects the protocol shape.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Who is actually broken right now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two examples I verified by downloading the packages rather than trusting a changelog. autogen-ext 0.7.5 declares &lt;code&gt;mcp&amp;gt;=1.11.0&lt;/code&gt; with no upper bound, imports &lt;code&gt;streamablehttp_client&lt;/code&gt;, unpacks the 3-tuple, and reads &lt;code&gt;inputSchema&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;isError&lt;/code&gt;, so its &lt;code&gt;[mcp]&lt;/code&gt; extra is broken against current &lt;code&gt;mcp&lt;/code&gt;, and as of mid-September that's still the case on main. llama-index-tools-mcp 0.5.0 is the same failure with a twist: it declared &lt;code&gt;mcp&amp;gt;=2.0.0&lt;/code&gt; but still unpacked the 3-tuple, so it was broken against its own dependency floor. Fixed in 0.5.1 in late August; 0.6.0 is current.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The pin that fixes it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you don't need 2.x features, pin back. The official 2.0.0 release notes tell library authors to keep a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;2&lt;/code&gt; upper bound, and the 1.x line is still getting security fixes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"mcp&amp;gt;=1.28,&amp;lt;2"&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="c"&gt;# resolves to 1.30.0 today&lt;/span&gt;
uv add &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"mcp&amp;lt;2"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the wrapper already fixed it (llama-index-tools-mcp &amp;gt;= 0.5.1, for example), upgrade the wrapper instead of downgrading &lt;code&gt;mcp&lt;/code&gt;. Read the release notes before you assume which side is fixed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before guessing, check what you're actually running:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"import importlib.metadata as m, inspect; print(m.version('mcp')); import mcp.client.streamable_http as sh; print(hasattr(sh,'streamablehttp_client')); print(inspect.signature(sh.streamable_http_client))"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And check the wrapper's &lt;code&gt;pyproject.toml&lt;/code&gt; for an unbounded &lt;code&gt;mcp&lt;/code&gt; dependency. That missing upper bound is the whole bug, and it'll happen again at the next major.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Two things that are not this bug
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I saw a couple of claims circulating while digging that can cost you hours if you chase them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An empty list from a tool producing zero content blocks is not a 2.x regression. I measured the same numbers on both 1.29.1 and 2.2.0: &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; gives 0 blocks, &lt;code&gt;''&lt;/code&gt; gives 1, &lt;code&gt;json.dumps([])&lt;/code&gt; gives 1, &lt;code&gt;['a','b']&lt;/code&gt; gives 2. It's long-standing FastMCP behavior in &lt;code&gt;_convert_to_content&lt;/code&gt;, which flattens a list item by item, and an empty list flattens to nothing. &lt;code&gt;structured_content&lt;/code&gt; is still correct. If your tool returns a list, return something wrapper-friendly instead of a bare &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; so the model gets one text block to read. That one matters if an agent keeps re-running your search instead of concluding "no match".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Separately, 2.x removed automatic return-value wrapping in the low-level &lt;code&gt;Server&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;mcp.types&lt;/code&gt; moved out into a &lt;code&gt;mcp-types&lt;/code&gt; package. Both are in the migration guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dependency major plus an unbounded requirement in the wrapper equals silent breakage at install time, with a stack trace pointing at your code instead of the dependency. Pin it, then skim the migration guide at py.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/migration. It's a long read, but the naming section alone saves the afternoon.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run a pile of MCP servers locally for agent work, and pinning is the boring habit that keeps it quiet. If you want to see how I aggregate and hot-swap those servers, that's in my Smart-MCP-Proxy repo, but the pin is the only part you need today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And I could be wrong about your specific stack, so test the pin in a throwaway venv first. That's what I did before writing any of this down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The finding that mattered in last year's assessment was not a vulnerability. The tester plugged a laptop into a socket in a meeting room, obtained an address, and from there opened a management port on the server that runs our payments processing. Nothing was exploited. Nothing needed to be. The point was that the path existed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We were not unaware of segmentation. We had virtual networks, we had a firewall between the offices and the data centre, and we had a diagram showing the two as separate zones. What the diagram did not show was a rule permitting the whole internal address range through that firewall, added in 2019 during a migration, with a comment saying it was temporary and the name of somebody who left in 2021. Underneath it were four hundred other rules, roughly a third of which nobody could explain and none of which anybody was willing to remove.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last part is the real subject. Segmentation is not technically difficult. It is difficult because the work is entirely downside. Nobody notices the attack that becomes harder, everybody notices the reporting tool that stops working on a Tuesday afternoon, and the engineer who removes a rule owns whatever breaks for a fortnight afterwards. So rules accumulate in one direction, and a network becomes flat by patience rather than by decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What made progress possible was giving up on segmenting everything. We picked the payments estate and the finance systems, logged every flow reaching them for six weeks before changing a single rule, and turned that log into a documented list of what is supposed to talk to them. Anything not on the list was denied, with the exceptions named, owned and dated. The camera system, the door controllers and the printers went onto their own segment, because none of them should ever have been able to reach a server and all of them run software their vendors last updated years ago.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We measure it with one number now: how many hosts on our network can open a connection to the payment systems. It was somewhere above four thousand. It is thirty-eight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Flat networks are not built. They are inherited, one reasonable exception at a time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;– Serguey Shinder&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Okay, quick thought experiment before we get into it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Someone hires you tomorrow. Not "in theory", like, you start Monday. What do you actually bring with you?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not your job title. Not the framework list sitting in your LinkedIn headline. I mean the real stuff, the thing a team gets when they get &lt;em&gt;you&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been chewing on this question for a while now, so let's talk it through.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Okay but why does this even matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the thing, anyone can slap "React, Node.js, PostgreSQL" on a CV. That's the easy part. It tells someone what you've &lt;em&gt;touched&lt;/em&gt;, but it says nothing about how you think, what you notice first when something's broken, or what kind of problems actually get you excited to open your laptop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The much more interesting question is quieter than a skills list: &lt;strong&gt;what do people come to you for?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Are you the one who gets handed the tangled legacy codebase nobody else wants to touch? The person who writes the docs nobody asked for, and then three weeks later it turns out that doc saved the whole team a day of confusion? The one who can take "make it feel more premium" from a client and actually turn that into a real list of changes?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of that fits neatly into a skills matrix. But honestly? It's usually the real reason someone wants you on their team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Alright, here's my honest answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not going to ask this and then dodge it myself, so here goes — this is what I'd bring:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;⚛️ &lt;strong&gt;Frontend development&lt;/strong&gt;:   React, Angular, TypeScript, modern JS. Not just turning a design into pixels, but actually thinking about state, performance, and what happens the moment the data doesn't behave like the happy path did in the demo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🐘 &lt;strong&gt;Backend development&lt;/strong&gt;:   PHP and Laravel. The logic and data layer the frontend is quietly leaning on the whole time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🔌 &lt;strong&gt;API development &amp;amp; integration&lt;/strong&gt;:  building REST APIs, and just as often, wrestling a third-party API into behaving the way its docs promised (it never quite does, does it?).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🌐 &lt;strong&gt;WordPress development&lt;/strong&gt;:  custom sites, plugins, e-commerce builds. Not glamorous, but a huge chunk of the actual web runs on this, and doing it well is a real skill, not a fallback.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🧩 &lt;strong&gt;Problem solving&lt;/strong&gt;:  taking a vague ask or a half-formed idea and turning it into something that actually works. Honestly, this might just be the whole job.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;⚡ &lt;strong&gt;Performance optimisation&lt;/strong&gt;:  caring not just about "does it work" but "does it feel good to use."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🧪 &lt;strong&gt;Testing&lt;/strong&gt;:  writing frontend tests people actually keep maintaining, instead of ones written once to hit a coverage number and never touched again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;📝 &lt;strong&gt;Technical writing&lt;/strong&gt;: tutorials and docs that developers actually want to read. Explaining something clearly is quietly one of the most valuable things you can do for a team.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🤖 &lt;strong&gt;AI-assisted development&lt;/strong&gt;:  using tools like Claude, ChatGPT, and Codex to research, build, debug, and review faster. Not a replacement for understanding the code, but a real boost on top of it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🤝 &lt;strong&gt;Collaboration&lt;/strong&gt;:  working well with other devs, product folks, and clients to actually ship the thing, not just produce code in a vacuum.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


And honestly, the biggest one: I'm still learning
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of the above means I've got it all figured out, far from it. The longer I do this, the more I realize how much I still don't know. And weirdly, that's one of my favorite parts of this whole field. There's always someone out there who's already solved the exact thing you're stuck on, in a way you never would've thought of.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's really what this post is about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Okay, your turn 👇
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you got hired tomorrow, what would &lt;em&gt;you&lt;/em&gt; bring?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not your job title. Not the tech list on your CV. The real stuff.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What are you actually good at?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What kind of problems do you enjoy untangling?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What's something you've learned that might genuinely help someone else on a team?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Drop it in the comments. And hey, if you're comfortable, throw your profile link in there too, maybe someone reading this is looking for exactly what you do. Maybe we end up collaborating, or trading advice, or just having a good conversation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's make the comments an actual place to connect, not just a list of stacks. 🚀&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the last few years I've been building a music tool that runs entirely in the browser: &lt;strong&gt;you play, what you played becomes readable notation, the notation plays back, and you hand it to someone.&lt;/strong&gt; That sentence took longer than I expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post isn't a feature tour. It's a record of the three places where the web said no:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Forking a notation engine (abc.js) to escape &lt;code&gt;innerHTML&lt;/code&gt; into a React structure — &lt;strong&gt;about a year&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Making a Track actually typeset — &lt;strong&gt;thousands of SVG nodes, and a cache that finally behaved&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Turning raw MIDI into &lt;em&gt;notes&lt;/em&gt; — &lt;strong&gt;about two years&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That third one happened around 2021. ChatGPT didn't exist and I never used Copilot. &lt;strong&gt;Every rule below was written by hand&lt;/strong&gt;, which turns out to matter by the end.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why a score roll, not a piano roll
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most DAWs give you a &lt;strong&gt;piano roll&lt;/strong&gt;: pitch vertical, time horizontal, rectangles in between. It's a good design, and it's easy to read — especially for beginners.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I went with a &lt;strong&gt;score roll&lt;/strong&gt; instead: you play, and &lt;em&gt;afterwards&lt;/em&gt; it becomes notation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not claiming notation is objectively better. There's research showing piano roll wins on comprehensibility for single-line melodies and for beginners, and I have no interest in arguing with it. My claim is narrower: &lt;strong&gt;something different happens cognitively.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Piano roll&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Score roll&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What you watch&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;where the note landed (monitoring space)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;music that became symbols (reading)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Strong at&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;monophony, nudging timing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;polyphony, two-hand roles, seeing a whole page at once&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How it feels&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;verifying data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;your performance answering you back as music&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And there's one constraint that made all of the following hard:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;No quantization.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your timing wobble never gets snapped to a grid, and the notation still has to be readable. Every problem below grows out of that one line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Wall 1: forking abc.js to escape &lt;code&gt;innerHTML&lt;/code&gt; (about a year)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Drawing notation glyphs from scratch — noteheads, stems, beams, accidentals — is not a weekend project. So I built on &lt;strong&gt;abc.js&lt;/strong&gt;, for one reason: the output is beautiful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the catch. Engines like this render to an &lt;strong&gt;SVG string&lt;/strong&gt;, and on the web that ends in a familiar line:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// This renders. And that's where it stops.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;container&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;innerHTML&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderAbc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;abcString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I didn't need to display notes. I needed you to &lt;strong&gt;grab a note and move it&lt;/strong&gt;. DOM injected through &lt;code&gt;innerHTML&lt;/code&gt; lives outside React, holds no state, and gets replaced wholesale on every re-render. As an editing surface, it's a dead end.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I forked it and read it. &lt;strong&gt;The work wasn't adding features — it was comprehension.&lt;/strong&gt; Where exactly does "layout result" (elements with coordinates) become final, and where does "assembling an SVG string" begin? Finding that seam took me close to a year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once I had the seam, the fix was boring, which is usually how you know it's the right one: &lt;strong&gt;steal the data before it becomes a string.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// simplified&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ScoreGlyph&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                       &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// stable id, so editing and re-rendering survive&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;kind&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;note&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;rest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;accidental&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;beam&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;slur&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;                        &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// SVG path&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// the engine now returns an array, not markup&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;glyphs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ScoreGlyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;layoutMeasure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;abcSource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ScorePathUnit&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;glyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onGrab&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;glyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ScoreGlyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;onGrab&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;glyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;transform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`translate(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;glyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;x&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;glyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;)`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onPointerDown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onGrab&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;glyph&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The pipeline it runs today:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;internal score model  →  ABC  →  forked abc.js  →  positioned elements  →  React components (editable)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I'd tell my past self:&lt;/strong&gt; the first job when you fork something isn't writing code, it's finding the point where you should let go. Cut in the wrong place and you inherit someone else's architecture forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Wall 2: typesetting a Track — SVG at scale, and what to cache
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The moment notes became React, the next wall showed up: &lt;strong&gt;count&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A score isn't one document, it's a grid of parts × measures. Show ten parts across thirty measures and you have hundreds of measure blocks in the viewport, each holding noteheads, stems, beams, accidentals, slurs. You reach thousands of SVG nodes without trying.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The naive version behaved exactly as badly as you'd expect:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;move one note, re-layout the entire piece&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;scrolling stutters&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;one React update ripples through the whole SVG tree&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision that fixed it was to &lt;strong&gt;stop rendering "a piece" at all&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// simplified&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CellKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;TrackId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;MeasureNumber&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// the unit of typesetting is one measure of one part&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1. skeleton (sync, cheap): decide measure widths and system breaks first&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;skeleton&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;buildSkeleton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// measureWidth[] / systemBreak&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2. cells (async, incremental): re-typeset only what changed&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;invalidate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CellKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;layoutCache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;                      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// drop the measure you touched, keep the rest&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then I clamped what exists on screen to the viewport plus a small margin, and cached layout in three tiers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tier&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Holds&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Policy&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hot&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;around the cursor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;always in memory&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Warm&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;recent chunks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;LRU with a byte ceiling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cold&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;local DB / storage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;re-fetch on demand&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part that actually mattered: &lt;strong&gt;the cache holds no SVG strings.&lt;/strong&gt; It holds layout results — coordinates and ids — and SVG gets rebuilt on demand. Keeping the markup around is the worst of both worlds: it costs memory and it's useless for editing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I'd tell my past self:&lt;/strong&gt; the web didn't fall over because SVG is heavy. It fell over because I handed the engine too much scope at once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Wall 3: teaching code to read a performance (about two years, in 2021, by hand)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the one that took years. A MIDI keyboard gives you this, and nothing else:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// events, not notes&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;noteOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;velocity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;88&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1917&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;noteOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;               &lt;span class="na"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2103&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Getting a length out of that is trivial:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// pitch -&amp;gt; start ticks&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onNoteOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;MidiEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onNoteOff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;MidiEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pitch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And then you realize &lt;strong&gt;that &lt;code&gt;duration&lt;/code&gt; is not a note.&lt;/strong&gt; Nobody plays an exact eighth. You get 461 ticks, then 503, then 478, and you have to turn that into notation a human can read — while refusing to quantize. The sound keeps the wobble. Only the &lt;em&gt;symbols&lt;/em&gt; get cleaned up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What actually stood in the way:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Timing&lt;/strong&gt; — which beat does this belong to? A late previous beat, or early syncopation into the next one?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Duration&lt;/strong&gt; — dotted? part of a tuplet? tied across the barline?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Overlap&lt;/strong&gt; — a new note arrives while the last one is still ringing: chord, or a second voice?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Chords&lt;/strong&gt; — "same tick" never happens. Human fingers don't land together.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two hands&lt;/strong&gt; — on a grand staff, is this note the right hand or the left?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chord detection ends up looking like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// not simultaneity — proximity&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isSimultaneous&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Note&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Note&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ticks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;chordTolerance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And hand assignment &lt;strong&gt;cannot&lt;/strong&gt; be a pitch boundary. "Below middle C is the left hand" dies on the first piece where the left hand crosses over, or where the melody sits low in the right. What it needs is continuity: which voice was moving where, what role the harmony is playing, where the line was heading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That pile of rules took about two years. It was 2021, there was no model to ask, and I used none — every rule written by hand, one broken case at a time. The upside is permanent: when the notation misreads a performance today, &lt;strong&gt;I know exactly which rule did it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What survived three years
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fork by reading first.&lt;/strong&gt; Where you cut matters more than the code you write.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;"Render everything" is the enemy on the web.&lt;/strong&gt; Shrink the scope you hand off, not the pixels.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cache the intermediate representation, never the markup.&lt;/strong&gt; Layout results are reusable; SVG strings are just expensive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human playing is messy — absorb it in the symbols, don't correct the human.&lt;/strong&gt; Refusing to quantize started as a constraint and became the character of the whole thing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Next: saying all of this in five languages
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Getting notation to work in a browser turned out to be half of shipping. The other half was saying the same thing in &lt;strong&gt;five languages&lt;/strong&gt; — the whole UI, plus 50+ pages of docs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where the next piece came from: writing &lt;code&gt;[[your copy]]&lt;/code&gt; in JSX, saving, and getting i18n keys — a tool called &lt;strong&gt;bracket-i18n&lt;/strong&gt;. The product came first; the tool fell out of it. That's the next post.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The editor in this post is called &lt;strong&gt;Imaju&lt;/strong&gt;, and it runs in a browser: &lt;a href="https://imaju.creathree.com/en/home" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://imaju.creathree.com/en/home&lt;/a&gt; — feel free to try breaking it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dashboard can look great in screenshots and still feel strange once you start clicking around.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That’s something I kept running into while working on StudioGrid Pro.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The individual screens weren’t really the problem. The problem was everything between them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you change a plan, the billing screen should know about it.&lt;br&gt;
If you update customer data, it shouldn’t feel like you moved into a completely different demo.&lt;br&gt;
Filters, states, settings and actions should make sense across the product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So lately I’ve been spending much more time on those connections than on adding more screens.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It’s a small difference when you look at a single screenshot, but a pretty big one when you actually use the UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also released a free Next.js SaaS dashboard starter while working on this, in case anyone wants to play with it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://studiogridpro.com/free/saas-dashboard-starter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://studiogridpro.com/free/saas-dashboard-starter&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Built with Next.js, React, TypeScript and Tailwind CSS.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Would be interested to know how other people handle this in templates or internal UI systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every SaaS team starts the same way: a simple boolean column in PostgreSQL (&lt;code&gt;has_seen_v2_modal&lt;/code&gt;), a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; hook, and a conditional modal component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fast forward 6 months, and your frontend bundle is 80KB heavier with 14 dead announcement modals, 3 hydration race conditions, and marketing asking why the banner didn't trigger for trial users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is why hardcoding in-app experiences is an anti-pattern—and how to architect a deterministic, decoupled announcement engine in Next.js.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The 3 Inevitable Failures of Hardcoded In-App UI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When engineering teams build announcement modals directly into application code, three major issues arise:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bundle Bloat&lt;/strong&gt;: Every new changelog modal, tooltip, or onboarding banner adds unused HTML/CSS to the initial page load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hydration Mismatch&lt;/strong&gt;: Checking &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; during SSR causes classic React hydration warnings or jarring layout flashes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deployment Friction&lt;/strong&gt;: Fixing a single typo in an announcement requires a full git commit, CI/CD pipeline run, and production deployment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Clean Solution: Deterministic Experience Slots
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of embedding static modals inside your page layouts, register a lightweight, polymorphic &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;ExperienceSlot /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; container. This container evaluates targeting rules (such as &lt;code&gt;page.path&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;user.plan&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;session.count&lt;/code&gt;) at runtime.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/dashboard/layout.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ExperienceSlot&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@neotic/react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;DashboardLayout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReactNode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"min-h-screen bg-slate-950 text-white"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* Dynamic, rule-evaluated announcement slot */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExperienceSlot&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="na"&gt;slotId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"dashboard-top-banner"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;daysActive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;daysActive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/dashboard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"p-8"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Because the client SDK is &lt;strong&gt;&amp;lt;4KB gzipped&lt;/strong&gt;, you get sub-millisecond local rule evaluation without dragging down your Core Web Vitals (LCP/INP).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Automating with AI Agents via Model Context Protocol (MCP)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The real breakthrough happens when you hook this up to your AI coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf). Instead of asking an AI agent to rewrite React components every time you launch a feature, you can connect an open Model Context Protocol (MCP) server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We set up &lt;a href="https://www.neotic.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Neotic&lt;/a&gt; with an open remote MCP endpoint (&lt;code&gt;https://www.neotic.app/api/mcp&lt;/code&gt;). Now, when you ship an update, you simply prompt your agent:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"Announce the new CSV Export feature to all Pro tier users on the &lt;code&gt;/reports&lt;/code&gt; route for the next 14 days."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent interacts with the MCP server to configure the deterministic JSON targeting rule, and it goes live instantly with zero frontend redeployments.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


💬 Let's Discuss
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How does your team currently manage in-app announcements and feature onboarding? Do you hardcode modals into React, use an external script, or automate via MCP? Drop your thoughts below!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;(If you're building with Next.js or AI coding agents, check out &lt;a href="https://www.neotic.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Neotic&lt;/a&gt; and our remote MCP endpoint at &lt;code&gt;https://www.neotic.app/api/mcp&lt;/code&gt;!)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When comparing Angular vs React for a new application, the short answer is that neither is automatically better. React is often a strong choice when flexibility, faster UI development, and a large ecosystem matter most, while Angular can be a better fit when you need a structured framework with built-in conventions for larger applications. The right decision depends on your project scope, team skills, development timeline, and long-term maintenance plans.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React offers flexibility and a broad ecosystem, making it easier to adapt the frontend stack to different project requirements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular provides a more structured, all-in-one framework that can reduce architectural decisions for larger teams.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React can help teams move quickly, but clear standards are important to avoid inconsistent code and unnecessary maintenance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular may require more upfront learning, but its conventions can make large applications easier to organize and maintain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The framework itself is rarely the biggest cost factor. Scope, integrations, testing, security, and long-term maintenance usually have a greater impact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The best choice should be based on your team, business requirements, scalability needs, and expected product lifecycle rather than popularity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why the Angular vs React decision matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choosing a frontend technology is not only a developer decision. It can affect how quickly your product reaches the market, how easily new developers can join the project, how consistently teams write code, and how much effort is required to maintain the application over time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For startups and growing businesses, the wrong choice can create unnecessary development work. A flexible stack without clear standards may become difficult to manage as the application grows. On the other hand, a highly structured framework can feel unnecessarily complex for a small application that needs to move quickly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The real question is not simply &lt;strong&gt;“Which is better, Angular or React?”&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;“Which one will help our team build, maintain, and scale this product with fewer problems?”&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That means looking beyond the first release and considering development speed, team experience, performance, security, integrations, hiring, and total cost of ownership.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Angular vs React: the practical difference
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is a component-based UI library that gives developers considerable freedom over how an application is structured. Teams can choose additional tools for routing, state management, data fetching, testing, and other requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular is a complete frontend framework with strong conventions and many capabilities included within its ecosystem. It provides tools and patterns for routing, forms, dependency injection, HTTP communication, testing, and application organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practical terms, the difference often comes down to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flexibility:&lt;/strong&gt; React gives teams more freedom to choose their tools, while Angular provides more built-in structure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Learning curve:&lt;/strong&gt; React can be easier to start with, while Angular may take longer to learn because of its broader framework concepts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Consistency:&lt;/strong&gt; Angular's conventions can make consistency easier across large teams. React requires teams to establish and enforce their own standards.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ecosystem:&lt;/strong&gt; React has a very broad ecosystem and many third-party solutions. Angular provides more functionality within its framework approach.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Development approach:&lt;/strong&gt; React lets teams build their own preferred architecture, while Angular provides a more defined path.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A simple way to think about it is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React gives you more freedom. Angular gives you more structure.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which framework saves more development time?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Development speed depends heavily on the team and project rather than the framework alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React can save time during early development because teams can choose only the tools they need and create reusable components quickly. It can be especially useful when the product requires frequent UI changes, experimentation, or custom user experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, flexibility can also create additional decisions. Teams need to decide which routing, state management, form, data-fetching, and testing solutions to use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular takes a more structured approach. Many common application requirements already have established patterns within the framework. This can reduce decision-making and help multiple developers follow the same architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a small team building a fast-moving product, React may provide a faster start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a large team building a complex application with many modules, Angular's structure may save time later by reducing architectural inconsistency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which one can reduce development costs?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The framework license itself is usually not the major cost factor. Development costs are influenced more by:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Project scope&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Number of features&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Third-party integrations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Backend complexity&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;UI and UX requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testing requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Security controls&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developer availability&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long-term maintenance&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React can be cost-effective when your team already has strong React experience and needs to deliver a product quickly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular can become cost-effective for larger applications where standardization and predictable architecture reduce long-term development and maintenance effort.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important thing is to consider &lt;strong&gt;total cost of ownership&lt;/strong&gt;, not just the cost of the first development phase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A framework that helps you launch quickly but creates significant refactoring later may not actually be the cheaper option.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


React vs Angular for scalability
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both React and Angular can support large applications. The bigger question is how the team manages architecture as the application grows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular's structured approach can be useful when multiple developers or teams work on the same application. Clear conventions around services, components, dependency injection, routing, and project organization can help reduce architectural drift.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React can also scale successfully, but teams generally need to establish stronger internal standards. A large React application benefits from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reusable component systems&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;TypeScript&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clear folder and architecture conventions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Shared UI libraries&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Standardized state management&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Consistent API patterns&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automated testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code quality rules&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Without these standards, different teams may solve the same problem in different ways, making the application harder to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For both frameworks, scalability ultimately depends more on &lt;strong&gt;architecture and engineering discipline&lt;/strong&gt; than the framework name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Performance: Angular vs React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Performance should be evaluated using the actual application rather than assuming one framework is always faster.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enterprise applications often contain dashboards, large tables, search filters, forms, charts, authentication flows, and API-heavy screens. These areas can become performance bottlenecks if they are not designed properly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With React, performance can be improved through practices such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Component optimization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code splitting&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lazy loading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Efficient state management&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Virtualized lists&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optimized API calls&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Avoiding unnecessary re-renders&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular applications can also achieve strong performance through:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lazy loading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Efficient change detection&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optimized RxJS usage&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ahead-of-Time compilation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Proper component architecture&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Efficient data handling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In both cases, poor application architecture can create performance problems regardless of the framework.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Security and maintenance considerations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security should be part of the framework decision from the beginning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A production application may need authentication, authorization, secure API communication, dependency management, input validation, logging, monitoring, and regular security updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular's structured approach can make it easier for teams to establish consistent patterns across a large codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is also suitable for secure applications, but teams have more responsibility for defining consistent patterns around authentication, routing, data handling, dependencies, and security controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Regardless of the framework, a mature development process should include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Secure authentication and authorization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dependency and vulnerability scanning&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Role-based access control&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Secure API communication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automated testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code reviews&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Logging and monitoring&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Regular dependency updates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The framework can support good security practices, but it cannot replace a proper security strategy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Hiring and team expertise
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another important factor is the people who will build and maintain the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React's broad ecosystem can make it easier for organizations to find developers with React experience in many markets. It also gives teams access to a large number of supporting libraries and tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular developers may be more specialized, but Angular's structured approach can be valuable for organizations that want developers to work within clearly defined patterns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before choosing a framework, ask:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Does our current team already know React or Angular?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which technology is easier for us to hire for?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Will the same team maintain the application for several years?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Will external developers or vendors contribute to the project?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Do we need strict architectural standards?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A framework that matches your existing team can often reduce onboarding time and development risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When should you choose React?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is often a strong choice when your project needs flexibility and fast iteration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider React when:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want to launch an MVP quickly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The UI will change frequently.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need a highly customized user experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your team already has strong React skills.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want freedom to select supporting tools.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You are building SaaS products, dashboards, portals, or customer-facing applications.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You expect different product teams to use different frontend patterns where appropriate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React is particularly useful when product teams need the freedom to experiment while still maintaining a well-defined development standard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When should you choose Angular?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular is often a strong choice when structure and consistency are major priorities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider Angular when:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You are building a large enterprise application.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple developers or teams will work on the same product.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want strong framework conventions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your application contains many forms, workflows, and business rules.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long-term maintainability is a major concern.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your organization prefers standardized development practices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want many common frontend capabilities within one framework ecosystem.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular can be especially useful when predictability is more important than having maximum freedom in technology choices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A simple framework decision checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of choosing based on popularity, evaluate both frameworks against your actual requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Choose React if:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Speed and flexibility are high priorities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your team already has React experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need frequent UI experimentation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want a broad ecosystem.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You are comfortable defining your own architecture.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Choose Angular if:
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your application is large and process-heavy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple teams need a consistent structure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You prefer an opinionated framework.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long-term governance is important.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want many common capabilities within one framework.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


If you are still unsure
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Build a small proof of concept using a real feature from your application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not compare the frameworks using a simple demo or counter application. Test something meaningful, such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Authentication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Role-based navigation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Complex forms&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;API integration&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Data tables&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Error handling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automated testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then compare development speed, code organization, performance, maintainability, and developer experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Common mistakes when choosing between Angular and React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One common mistake is choosing React simply because it is popular. React's flexibility is powerful, but without clear standards, different developers can introduce different libraries and architectural patterns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another mistake is choosing Angular only because it is considered an enterprise framework. A structured framework does not automatically make an application well-designed. Architecture, testing, security, and code quality still matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A third mistake is focusing only on the initial development cost. The real cost of an application includes future maintenance, upgrades, bug fixing, developer onboarding, infrastructure, and feature development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, companies sometimes ignore the existing team's skills. Moving to a technology that nobody on the team knows well can increase the initial learning curve and project risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best framework is the one that fits your &lt;strong&gt;business goals, technical requirements, team capabilities, and long-term roadmap&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Which is better for business applications: React or Angular?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both can be excellent choices for business applications. React is often preferred when flexibility and rapid UI development are important, while Angular can be a strong choice for applications that require structure, consistency, and long-term maintainability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is React cheaper than Angular?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not necessarily. React can reduce initial development time for teams that already have React experience, but long-term costs depend on architecture, integrations, testing, maintenance, and team productivity. Angular may provide cost benefits for large applications through its standardized structure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is Angular harder to learn than React?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular generally has a broader learning curve because developers need to understand more framework concepts and conventions. React can be easier to start with, although building a large React application still requires knowledge of additional tools and architectural patterns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Which is better for large-scale applications?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both can handle large-scale applications. Angular can be particularly suitable when multiple teams need a consistent architecture, while React works well when organizations want flexibility and have strong engineering standards in place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How should a company decide between React and Angular?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with your business requirements, existing team skills, application complexity, expected growth, integrations, security requirements, and long-term maintenance plans. A small proof of concept using real application requirements can also help make the decision more confidently.&lt;/p&gt;




